{C}

The experimental data demonstrate that the ADC (anti-TNF-GRM) is markedly more effective than anti-TNF at suppressing inflammation (ear/paw swelling) at the matched 10 mg/kg dose across Experiments 1 and 2, while also producing substantially less bone-density loss (−0.3 vs −0.75 mm³) in Experiment 3. GRM alone is likewise active, but its bone-density arm was deliberately run at only 3 mg/kg, precluding any verified claim about side-effect ranking versus the ADC at equal doses.  

Options A, B, D, F, G, and H contain direct factual contradictions (wrong efficacy ordering, false “same risk” assertions, or incorrect statements about GRM inactivity). Options E and I assert that the chosen doses permit clean side-effect comparisons or support untested extrapolations for GRM at 10 mg/kg; both are unsupported by the design. Only C is free of error or unsupported inference.

